"Shows how autonomous participants coordinate business processes through messages across explicit organizational boundaries.\n\nUse when: Clarify supplier, buyer and carrier responsibilities. Find missing requests, responses and timeout handling between parties.\n\nAvoid when: Only one participant's internal procedure needs explanation.\n\nVisual grammar: Pools identify independent participants and may hide internals. Sequence flows order activities inside each pool. Dashed message flows connect distinct participants, never activities inside one pool.\n\nBuild: List participants and define the transaction under discussion. Draw each visible process independently with its own events. Connect named messages and check sender, receiver and timing.\n\nCheck: Each message has a valid sender and recipient. Sequence arrows stay inside their participant pool. Every expected response has a receiving event or task.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing sequence arrows between organizations. Assuming a message sent means the receiver completed its work.\n\nExample: A retailer sends an order to a supplier, receives confirmation and exchanges a separate dispatch message with a carrier.\n\nAlternatives: BPMN process, BPMN choreography."
